"},{"path":"README.md","role":"territory-readme","digest":"sha256:967ff83af29997aa090e269f9bed500c3a7bd8030557b2e93c932aac9181f00a","bytes":6170,"truncated":false,"text":"# free-compute-dev

An autonomous software-development harness built on the firm's stated economics:

- **Default effort = contributed compute.** Patch generation and iteration run on
  the cheapest sufficient endpoint currently admitted by LISS (today Groq, Gemini,
  GitHub Models, OpenRouter when available, and later private GPU workers).
- **`claude -p` is the gated exception**, not the default — used as a quality gate and
  for problems above a difficulty threshold, because it is the expensive resource.

This is a persistent software-production fabric, not a conversational coding
wrapper. A caller can submit an objective and leave. The fabric observes the
territory, contracts parallel inference, executes bounded tools, verifies
candidates, integrates one without overwriting concurrent work, and records the
successful trajectory for reuse.

## Live result

On 2026-07-19, three isolated workers contracted Groq, Gemini, and GitHub Models
through the persistent LISS broker. They made 17 useful tool decisions over eight
waves. Gemini independently navigated to `src/executor.mjs`, derived a two-edit
repair for a real temporary-directory leak, passed the package suite in
isolation, and the runtime atomically integrated and reverified it. No interactive
model call supplied the patch. The first run also taught the deterministic carrier
to accept providers' native JSON action dialects and structured `{search,replace}`
edit arrays.

The next unattended queued mission exposed a task-class capability boundary: three
cheap actors could not author a semantically valid early-quorum regression. The
bounded residue was resubmitted through the queue to Claude with provider-enforced
JSON Schema. Claude inspected the test and runtime, authored the regression, and
the queue integrated it after verification. The resulting 26-test suite proves
early quorum, dead-worker claim abandonment, protected-border integration, and the
durable carrier itself. The failed cheap trajectory remains evidence for future
task-class pricing instead of being hidden.

## Operating surfaces

- `run-union-dev-mission mission.json` runs one objective through parallel isolated
  tool workers and a verification/integration gate.
- `run-union-dev-program program.json` asks multiple cloud planners for acyclic
  mission DAGs, validates them, and executes the cheapest valid plan until the
  declared program acceptance command passes.
- `submit-union-dev-mission mission.json` and `run-union-dev-worker` provide a
  durable queue, claims, heartbeats, stale-claim recovery, and unattended service.
- `abandon-union-dev-mission exact-id reason` terminally settles work whose worker
  was deliberately stopped, preventing stale recovery from purchasing obsolete
  inference again.
- LISS's persistent broker accepts batches through a filesystem spool as well as a
  Unix socket. Sandboxed workers need neither network access nor provider keys;
  they atomically signal jobs through the spool while the one broker owns cloud
  access, pricing, concurrency, replay, and the append-only settlement journal.

Each worker can `list_files`, `search`, `read`, apply exact edits, run the declared
verification/acceptance gates, and invoke only commands pre-admitted by the mission.
Candidate workspaces are copies; integration compares source digests and classifies
a concurrent source change as a protected border instead of overwriting it.

An unresolved base phase is automatically re-contracted as `software-engineering`
work requiring schema-validated actions. LISS then quotes only suppliers with that
capability instead of repeatedly buying cheap syntactic completions. Successful
base→escalation routes are appended by task class; a later mission of that class
starts at the witnessed sufficient stage unless its manifest explicitly refuses
learned routing. Program-DAG missions use the same pipeline.

Execution-passing candidates are not integrated on that evidence alone. Unless a
mission explicitly declines semantic review, three independently sharded cloud
reviewers compare the changed source to the original objective and constraints.
A valid-review majority is required. Rejected candidates remain isolated, and the
review reasons become residue for a second software-engineering revision stage.
Declared writable paths are enforced by the tool carrier rather than trusted to a
prompt.

## The solve loop ([src/solve-loop.mjs](src/solve-loop.mjs))

MAPE-K over a working tree:

1. **Route** — if an upfront difficulty estimate is at/above the threshold, skip free
   rounds and escalate straight to `claude -p` (don't waste rounds on research-hard work).
2. **Generate → Apply → Test** — up to `maxFreeRounds` on free inference; each failed
   round feeds its test output back as context (this is the Analyze→Plan feedback).
3. **Escalate** — if free rounds are exhausted without passing tests, escalate to
   `claude -p`, then re-run tests to confirm.
4. Return `solved-free | solved-escalated | failed` with a full round log.

Every dependency (`generatePatch`, `applyPatch`, `runTests`, `escalate`, `difficulty`) is
injectable, so the escalation *policy* is tested deterministically with mocks
([test/solve-loop.test.mjs](test/solve-loop.test.mjs)) without spending real inference.

## Recursive improvement

Every run appends its tool/result trajectory. Later missions recall similar verified
actions. Structural action fingerprints accumulate by task class; after repeated
high-verification recurrence, the promotion readout marks the class eligible to become
a deterministic resolver. Provider dialect failures are rectified in the carrier, so a
new cycle inherits what prior cycles learned rather than requiring another prompt.

## Default implementations

- Inference: [src/fabric-client.mjs](src/fabric-client.mjs) — batches work through the
  LISS spool/socket/dispatcher boundary; provider selection remains LISS-owned.
- Executor: [src/executor.mjs](src/executor.mjs) — `git apply` a unified diff, run a test
  command, capture exit/stdout/stderr.
- Escalation: [src/escalate.mjs](src/escalate.mjs) — spawn `claude -p` in the work tree.
"},{"path":"src/edits.mjs","role":"relative-import-interface","digest":"sha256:b532c337a2910f418b689131e93ac808efce7b6605605f0d141b556de7fbe177","bytes":2074,"truncated":false,"text":"// SEARCH/REPLACE edit blocks — the proven patch format (2026-07-18: a capable free model,
// Groq Llama-70B, produced a correct edit for a real bounty this way; unified diffs and the
// tiny model did not). More reliable for free models than unified diffs, and applied by
// deterministic string replacement instead of fragile hunk headers.
//
// TOLERANT by design: models often write the file path after `<<<<<<<` instead of "SEARCH",
// so the parser accepts any suffix on the marker lines.

export const EDIT_SYSTEM_PROMPT =
  "You output ONLY edit blocks, nothing else. Each edit block is EXACTLY:\n" +
  "<<<<<<< SEARCH\n(exact existing lines, copied verbatim)\n=======\n(replacement lines)\n>>>>>>> REPLACE\n" +
  "The SEARCH text must match the file byte-for-byte. Include ONLY the exact lines you are changing plus minimal surrounding context — NEVER whole files, NEVER unchanged import lines. The REPLACE must not duplicate any line that already exists outside the SEARCH region.";

export function buildEditMessages({ task, filePath, source, failures = [] }) {
  const fail = failures.map((f) => f.test || f.apply).filter(Boolean).slice(-2).join("\n");
  return [
    { role: "system", content: EDIT_SYSTEM_PROMPT },
    { role: "user", content: `${task.title || ""}\n${task.body || ""}\n\nFile ${filePath}:\n${source}${fail ? `\n\nPrior failed attempts:\n${fail}` : ""}` },
  ];
}

// Apply one or more edit blocks to `source`. Returns { ok, count, result?, reason? }.
export function applyEditBlocks(source, text) {
  const re = /<<<<<<<[^\n]*\n([\s\S]*?)\n=======[^\n]*\n([\s\S]*?)\n>>>>>>>[^\n]*/g;
  let out = source, count = 0, m;
  while ((m = re.exec(String(text || "")))) {
    const search = m[1];
    const replace = m[2];
    if (search.trim() === "") { out = replace; count++; continue; } // empty SEARCH = whole-file replace
    if (out.includes(search)) { out = out.replace(search, replace); count++; continue; }
    return { ok: false, count, reason: "SEARCH block not found verbatim" };
  }
  return { ok: count > 0, count, result: out };
}
